In Java, to use a `HashSet`, you need to import it from the `java.util` package. Here's the basic syntax:

```java
import java.util.HashSet;

HashSet<Integer> mySet = new HashSet<>();
```

This creates a new `HashSet` that stores integers. The angle brackets `<Integer>` specify the type of elements the set will hold.

To add elements to the set, you use the `.add()` method:
```java
mySet.add(5);
mySet.add(10);
```

To check if an element exists in the set, you use the `.contains()` method:
```java
if (mySet.contains(5)) {
    // do something
}
```

To iterate over the elements in a set, you can use an enhanced for loop:
```java
for (Integer num : mySet) {
    System.out.println(num);
}
```

Note that `HashSet` doesn't maintain insertion order, so if you need sorted output, you'll need to copy the elements to a sorted data structure like `ArrayList` and then sort it using `Collections.sort()`.

